+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|               PrimeTime and DesignCompiler SDF Consistency                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
Due to differences in how PrimeTime and DesignCompiler handle state-dependent
delays and edge-triggered devices, the SDF generated by these 2 tools may be
inconsistent.

Due to these inconsistencies, SDF created by PrimeTime, may fail to
back-annotate timing to the Artisan Verilog and/or VHDL models. To correct
back-annotation problems, please run the "pt_postprocessor.pl" on your SDF
file(s) before attempting back-annotation. Command line usage for
"pt_postprocessor.pl" is as follows:

  pt_postprocessor.pl -s <input_sdf_filename> -o <output_sdf_filename>

The modified SDF file will be printed to stdout.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|               PrimeTime 2002.09 and multi-Voltage Libraries                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
When mixing Artisan standard cell libraries with other IP libraries that are
characterized for a different nominal voltage (such as the case when IO pad
nominal voltage does not match standard cell nominal voltage), you may
encounter negative net delays and RC-005 warnings during RC delay calculation.
It is also possible that PrimeTime may calculate timing incorrectly without
reporting a warning during delay calculation without SPEF.

In absence of an SPEF, PrimeTime will scale delay values in and out of the IO
cells based on the multi-voltage delay scaling; however, the standard cell
library is characterized under the assumption that there is no external delay
scaling.

To correct for these problems, in PrimeTime set:

 set lib_thresholds_per_lib   false
 set timing_prelayout_scaling false
